// Target-scoped signing settings for the Big4Watchability app target ONLY.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (2026-09-21, CI run #49):
// build-ios-testflight.yml used to pass CODE_SIGN_STYLE,
// PROVISIONING_PROFILE_SPECIFIER, CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY and DEVELOPMENT_TEAM as
// arguments on the `xcodebuild archive` command line. Command-line build
// settings are GLOBAL - they apply to every target in the build graph, not
// just the app. That was harmless for eleven straight dispatches because the
// project had no dependencies. The moment Firebase, GoogleMobileAds and UMP
// were added, the archive failed with a wall of:
//
//   GoogleUtilities_GoogleUtilities-Environment does not support
//   provisioning profiles ... but provisioning profile *** has been
//   manually specified.
//
// Swift Package targets cannot take a provisioning profile, and there is no
// way to scope a command-line setting to one target. An xcconfig attached to
// a single target via XcodeGen's `configFiles` is scoped by construction, so
// the package targets are left completely alone.
//
// THIS FILE IS INTENTIONALLY EMPTY IN THE REPOSITORY. The real values are
// written into it at runtime by the TestFlight workflow, from GitHub
// secrets, immediately before the archive step - they are never committed.
// Leaving it empty also means the unsigned CI build (build-ios.yml, which
// passes CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO) is entirely unaffected by it.

// NOTE: CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY is deliberately NOT written here. XcodeGen adds a
// target-level signing preset (CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY = iPhone Developer) and
// target settings outrank an xcconfig, so setting it in this file has no
// effect - proven by run #29, where this file said "Apple Distribution" and
// xcodebuild still resolved "iPhone Developer". It lives in project.yml's
// target settings instead, as an [sdk=iphoneos*] conditional. Only the two
// values that are actually secret are written here.
